) obtained. Those who are bound in fetters, those who are about to be bound, those struck by the various sufferings of water, fire, and the like—through this [<s>hṛdaya</s> (heart-dhāraṇī)] I become the shelter, protection, refuge, and final resort for all sentient beings. With this <s>hṛdaya</s> I shall draw all wicked <s>yakṣas</s> and <s>rākṣasas</s>, make them possess minds of loving-kindness and compassion, and establish them in unsurpassed perfect awakening (<s>anuttarā samyaksaṃbodhi</s>). Thus, O <s>Bhagavat</s>, is this <s>hṛdaya</s> of mine of great supernatural power.
By reciting it even once, the four root transgressions (<s>mūlāpattayaḥ</s>) are destroyed, and the five actions of immediate retribution (<s>pañcānantarya-karmas</s>) will be completely attenuated. How much more so for those who practice it as prescribed!
There will be wholesome roots planted with hundreds of thousands of <s>Buddhas</s>. For those who merely hear it—how much more for those who practice recitation (<s>japa</s>) and <s>sādhana</s>! I shall fulfill all wishes. And whoever observes a fast on the fourteenth or fifteenth [of the month] dedicated to me—for forty thousand <s>kalpas</s> (aeons) they will turn away from <s>saṃsāra</s>. O <s>Bhagavat</s>, by the mere grasping of that name, it is equal to more than a hundred thousand <s>koṭis</s> and <s>niyutas</s> of <s>Buddhas</s>. By grasping my name, all sentient beings attain non-retrogression (<s>avaivartika</s>). They are freed from all diseases, from all obscurations, from all fears, from all evil conduct of body, speech, and mind. For them, <s>Buddha</s>-awakening will be in the palm of their hands (
